ram (FDLP).  aGPO Historic Shelflist Project- publication not in hand.  aSL Li 1-5-90.  aSL Li 2-6-90.20aSociety of Federal Labor Relations Professionals (U.S.)0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n852706001 aUnited States.bFederal Service Impasses Panel.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n810111911 aUnited States.bFederal Labor Relations Authority.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n79104064  aC0bGPO  aHistoric Shelflist Drawer 788; HSL004;c20260820  aHistoric Shelflist Drawer 788; HSL004; 20260909  aVEND01b2026082602909cam a2200481 i 00147834320260908080125.0m     o  d f      cr |||||||||||240917s2024    coua   fs    f000 0 eng c8 a2447464qOSTI ID  a(OCoLC)1456157303  aSOEbengerdaepncSOEdSOEdOCLCQdGPOdOCLCOdGPO  apcc  an-us---  aGPOO  a0430-P-06 (online)0 aE 9.19:NREL/FS-5000-8768300a2022 Component Innovation Awardee :bPecos Wind Power.3 aTwo thousand twenty-two Component Innovation Awardee : Pecos Wind Power 1aGolden, CO :bNational Renewable Energy Laboratoryc2024  a1 online resource (2 pages) :bcolor illustrations.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNREL/FS ;v5000-87683  aThis is a publication issued by the United States federal government or produced with federal funds.  aIn scope of the U.S. Government Publishing Office Cataloging and Indexing Program (C&I) and Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP).  a"September 2024."  a"Pecos Wind Power SUB-2023-10334"--Page 2.  aPublicly released3 aDistributed wind energy systems supply electricity for nearby homes and businesses, often in remote and rural regions. Systems capable of capturing energy in areas with low wind speeds make it possible to deploy these clean energy systems in many geographic locations. Pecos Wind Power's 85-kilowatt (kW) horizontal-axis PW85 wind turbine shows potential to reduce the levelized cost of energy by approximately 55% when compared to typical small wind systems while achieving utility-scale capacity factors of more than 40% in areas with lower wind speeds. To maximize cost competitiveness, the Pecos Wind Power turbine needs a blade optimized for low wind speeds. Through its 2022 Competitiveness Improvement Project (CIP) award, Pecos Wind Power and partner Wetzel Wind Energy Services will model, optimize, fabricate, and validate a 14.5-meter wind turbine blade made specifically for these applications. Three prior CIP awards supported Pecos Wind Power's initial development of the PW85 turbine.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF caption (NLR website, viewed Aug. 24, 2026). 0aWind power.2 aNational Renewable Energy Laboratory (U.S.),eissuing body. 0aNREL/FS ;v5000-87683.40uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo259336704 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://docs.nlr.gov/docs/fy24osti/87683.pdf  aC0bGPO  abca29 20260908  abca29 20260824b20260824  aBIBCONEWb20260824  aVEND01b2026082601949cam a2200469 i 00147835720260908080252.0m     o  d f      cr |||||||||||260824s2024    coua   fs    f000 0 eng c  a(OCoLC)1612835869  aGPObengerdaepncGPOdGPOdOCLCOdGPO  apcc  an-us---  aGPOO  a0430-P-06 (online)0 aE 9.19:NREL/FS-5500-886541 aUnited States.bDepartment of Energy.bOffice of Energy Efficiency and Renewable Energy,eauthor.10aAdvanced manufactured home case study :ba collaboration for high-efficiency, affordable housing. 1aGolden, CO :bNational Renewable Energy Laboratoryc2024  a1 online resource (5 pages) :bcolor illustrations.  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier1 aNREL/FS ;v5500-88654  aThis is a publication issued by the United States federal government or produced with federal funds.  aIn scope of the U.S. Government Publishing Office Cataloging and Indexing Program (C&I) and Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP).  a"December 2024"--Page 5.  aDOE/GO-10202